Why we should reuse threads

Say we have a program where we have multiple threads. It reads file from local disk line by line, processes each line
and writes processed lines in a new file.

For each file, we create a thread and when processing of that file is complete, thread will be destroyed.

Question is, if we have 10,000 files, should we create 10,000 threads?

Lets see what happens when we create one thread from resource perspective.

When we create 1 thread, JVM allocates 1MB Stack Size for that thread. This is configurable as vm arg.
If we create 10k threads, there will be 1GB of memory allocated only to sustain the stack areas of those threads.
If we reach this memory boundary, JVM will stop with out of memory exception

We may also hit performance limit due to context switch overhead by the scheduler. Threads will spend more time doing the context switch rather than
actual work. 

How do we solve this issue ? Process large number of tasks 

Solution is to reuse the threads, we need threads to perform many tasks. Add limited num of threads in our app as per cpu cores and workload type.

They will have to be long running, while loop to wait for work to be submitted.
Build a way to submit work to the threads, good way to do this is to have a task queue.
Threads will fetch task from queue and do work. This is fundamental idea of thread pool.
Thread pool is collection of thread connected to pool to which it will get work to execute.



ThreadPoolExecutor

- ThreadPoolExecutor tpe = new ThreadPoolExecutor(corePoolSize, maxPoolSize, Timeunit, workqueue(runnables))
- corePoolSize - num of worker threads 
- thread pool can expand and shrink based on how much work is in the queue
- task not returning any results -> tpe.execute(runable) 
- Future<V> future = tpe.submit(callable) - returns results. Callable has single method call() that returns a generic type
- future.get() - if result is avail, return it, if not, wait until it has result
- future.isDone(), future.isCancelled()
- IMP: Even after executing all the tasks, the thread pool would still continue to wait for other tasks to be added to thread pool as it has 
user level threads and Java application exits only when all user level threads are destroyed either by interrupted or by terminating execution.
- So we need to destroy all threads before app can stop. Otherwise app will nto start until you force kill the process.
- For that, we have tpe.shutdown(), tpe.shutdownNow()
- shutdown is graceful way of closing - first stop accepting new tasks, execute all the active tasks and all tasks in queue. 
- shutdownNow is forceful way - stop accepting new task, send interrupt to all worker threads, returns all tasks currently in queue.
- shutdown will not wait for threads to complete, otherwise use awaitTermination(timeout) 
- tpe uses lazy execution, if we don't send any task no threads will be created, depending on tasks, it will create the threads. 
- tpe.prestartAllCoreThreads() - create all threads as per core pool size 


Different queue types to use with TPE
- Blocking Queue - block if full or nothing in queue
- type of queue will directly impact performance of tp
- ArrayBlockingQueue - queue backed by array and a blocking queue. It is bounded queue in terms of num of elements that can be added to queue.
- If we try to add more tasks than the max num of threads can handle, and if queue reaches its capacity, the task will be rejected.
- unbounded queues - no capacity setup upfront - LinkedBlockingQueue. (capacity is optional else by default it will use int max value)
- whenever we add tasks, they will always be queued. This may lead to oom exceptions due to too many tasks
- In high throughput app, we may not want to keep huge num of tasks in memory
- SynchronousQueue - pass task directly to thread by calling tpe.submit(). If no worker thread avail, it will simply reject task.


Handling Exceptions in tpe
- Lets say a task throws unchecked exception - runtime exception. 
- 3 ways to handle it - surround the code which can throw the exception with try catch Exception. 
- Submit returns a future, when we call future.get() it will throw Interrupted or ExecutionException and ExecutionExcepton is a catchall
for any kind of exception so we can handle that during future.get()
- Create custom tpe class extends Tpe and override specific method afterExecute(), tpe will call this method for each task executed by worker thread.
- afterExecute(Runnable r, Throwable t) if t is null that means the task was executed successfully. 
- if we use submit, exeption is captured in future and won't be avail in afterExecute() and it won't call this method with non-null throwable


Future<T> future = tpe.submit(() -> throw new RuntimeException("test"));
try {
future.get();
} catch (ExecutionException e){}

recommended way is to handle it inside runnable where the code executes, best way to debug it. 


Managing Rejected Tasks in tpe
- all worker threads are busy and size of pool has reached max and queue is full
- one way to handle is surrount submit with try catch and handle rejected exception
- use overloaded tpe constrcutor that takes a RejectedExecutionHandler and override the method for handling logic
- there are also some policy available with tpe in constructor



















